; regression test for a real bug found while investigating `let x, %A` (mixed
; statement/term variables) for doc/kurt-soundness.md: `remove_outer_forall_quantifiers`
; strips an explicit outer `forall` and replaces its bound variable with a fresh internal
; name, but it called `new_var_name()` unconditionally -- even when the bound variable was
; a `%`-prefixed *statement* schema variable, which should get a fresh `%%NN`-style statement
; variable name instead (exactly as `rename_all_vars_rec`'s own binder-handling branch already does
; correctly, a few lines below in the same file). The mismatch silently turned an explicitly
; `forall`-quantified statement variable into a term variable the moment it was stored.
;
; this is not just a classification nitpick: `impl_elim`'s case for a bare statement-variable
; premise (matching the *whole* conclusion unconditionally, see doc/kurt-soundness.md #6) is
; gated on `is_bool_var_token`, which checks exactly this classification. So an axiom written
; with an explicit `forall %A (...)` -- a natural, idiomatic way to quantify over a whole
; proposition -- silently stopped being usable via that mechanism, a false rejection. Confirmed
; by direct comparison against the exact same axiom written without the explicit `forall`
; (`use %A implies P`, relying on implicit universal quantification of free variables), which
; was never affected since it never goes through `remove_outer_forall_quantifiers` at all.
;
; Since 2026-10-20 binders range over terms: `forall %A ...` is a type error, and the schema with a
; free `%A` is the only way to say "for every statement" -- which is what this test now checks.
load logic
prop P, Q
expect "TypeError" "a binder ranges over terms"
    use forall %A (%A implies P)
use %A implies P
show P "boolean schema variable must stay boolean after its explicit forall is stripped"
proof
  Q implies P
  P
qed
